First create image using dockerfile and app.js
   ---check these are running or not 
        --- Docker installer
        --- running docker desktop
        --- docker registry
        --- kubernetes version
        --- minikube status
Either you can use clone git Repositry or use directly
## Create a file called 'app.js'
markdown
```javascript
// app.js
const http = require("http");

http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.end("Multi-stage Dockerfile practice");
}).listen(3000);

## create a file called dockerfile
dockerfile
# Stage 1: Build
FROM node:18-alpine AS builder
WORKDIR /app
COPY package.json .
RUN npm install
COPY . .

# Stage 2: Runtime
FROM node:18-alpine
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=builder /app /app
EXPOSE 3000
CMD ["node", "app.js"]

docker build -t lokesh815/my-first-docker-image1:latest .

docker run -it lokesh815/my-first-docker-image

login to docker registry

docker push lokesh815/my-first-docker-image:latest

After successfully create image check for kubernetes 

kubectl version

Next minikube status
minikube start( it runs in docker desktop driver if you don't have any driver like virtualbox..)
if you have virtualbox
minkube start --memory=4096 --driver=virtualbox

## create deployment.yml file 
vim deployment.yml
yml 
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: demo-deployment
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: demo-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: demo-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: demo-container
        image: lokesh815/my-first-docker-image1:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 3000

kubectl apply -f deployment.yml

---check 
 kubectl get deployments
 kubectl get pods

create service
To access your app outside the cluster,expose it via Nodeport (or) LoadBalancer.....

## create service.yml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: demo-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: demo-app
  type: NodePort
  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 3000
      targetPort: 3000

kubectl apply -f service.yml

--check service 
kubectl get svc

Access app 

minikube service demo-service
This open app local browser automatically
if its not working and says like ! Because you are using a Docker driver on windows, the terminal needs to be open to run it.

try this 
kubectl port-forward service/demo-service 3000:3000

update deployment
  if you push a new image version (eg:2)

kubectl set image deployment/demo-deployment demo-container=lokesh815/my-first-docker-image:2
kubectl rollout status deployment/demo-deployment

Rollback (if needed)
kubectl rollout undo deployment/demo-deployment

